Fixed. Here's what was wrong and what I did:

**Problem:** The `pip` package was corrupted — the actual `pip/` package directory had been deleted from `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/`, but the `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory remained. This made it look like pip was installed (metadata was there), but the code was gone, so every `pip` command crashed with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`.

**Fix:** I removed the stale `.dist-info` directory and re-bootstrapped pip using `python3 -m ensurepip --default-pip`, which restored the full pip package. Both `pip3` and `python3 -m pip` now work correctly.

